WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:18.000
THE STATE OF GOOGLE FIBER:
Remember Google Fiber? Way back in 2010,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:23.160
Google sought to win the broadband internet&nbsp;
market by introducing revolutionary internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:30.120
speeds of 1 Gbps. They were looking to achieve&nbsp;
this by introducing fiber optic technology to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:37.380
the general market. This was quite a bold move&nbsp;
as the legacy players like AT&amp;T, Comcast, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:43.500
Charter had such a stronghold on the market. But,&nbsp;
if anyone was capable of taking on these giants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:49.260
it was someone like Google. Things started off&nbsp;
strong for Google Fiber as they launched in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:56.040
Kansas followed by Texas, Utah, and Tennessee.&nbsp;
It seemed like Google was doing it. They were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.040 --> 00:01:02.100
putting legacy players to shame as people flocked&nbsp;
to Google’s offering. But, this success didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:08.400
last too long. In the mid 2010s, AT&amp;T would&nbsp;
start hitting back and they would flex just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:14.520
how powerful they truly were. While Google Fiber&nbsp;
was expanding city by city, AT&amp;T would announce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:21.240
a nationwide rollout covering dozens of cities.&nbsp;
And they weren’t bluffing either given that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:29.340
would spend on an eye watering $140 billion to&nbsp;
make this vision a reality. This was obviously a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:34.500
massive morale hit for Google, and they would soon&nbsp;
announce that they were cutting their fiber staff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:41.580
by half. To make things worse, AT&amp;T and Charter&nbsp;
would start sabotaging Google Fiber by suing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:47.400
them and pulling political favors. It seems like&nbsp;
all of this pressure was too much for CEO, Craig&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:53.040
Barratt to handle as he would step down during the&nbsp;
worst of it. But, the nail in the coffin didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:58.560
come till the late 2010s by which point AT&amp;T had&nbsp;
finished much of their critical rollout. Suddenly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:04.620
Google was no longer introducing fiber to new&nbsp;
areas. Many of the cities that they were expanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:12.240
to already had fiber thanks to AT&amp;T. And it didn’t&nbsp;
help that AT&amp;T was not only cheaper but more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:17.760
reputable from the general public’s perspective.&nbsp;
With no choice left, Google would actually begin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:23.160
to leave certain cities that they had pioneered&nbsp;
like Louisville Kentucky. Since then, we haven’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:28.680
heard much news from Google. Apparently, they’re&nbsp;
still expanding and have plans of moving into 5&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:34.620
new states, but it’s not clear how much of&nbsp;
this will come to fruition especially with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:40.440
the recession. Objectively speaking, it looks like&nbsp;
Google bit off way more than they could chew and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:45.900
they got put in their place. But, what if I told&nbsp;
you that Google knew that this would happen from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:52.380
the very beginning, and this wasn’t their worst&nbsp;
case scenario. It was actually the scenario that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:58.380
they were betting on. So, here’s why Google Fiber&nbsp;
was actually Google’s most successful failure.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:06.180
STAGNANT PLAYERS:
To understand why Google entered the broadband&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:11.880
market as a whole, we’ll have to take a look back&nbsp;
at the state of fiber internet before Google. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:18.420
see, fiber optic data transmission is by no means&nbsp;
a new technology. It was actually demonstrated way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:25.560
back in 1965 by a German physicist named Manfred&nbsp;
Borner. And NASA even used fiber optic cables&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:30.480
in the cameras that they sent to the moon. At&nbsp;
first, the technology wasn’t commercially viable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:35.520
given that it required an extremely intensive&nbsp;
manufacturing process. High quality optical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:42.540
fibers could barely be manufactured at 2 meters&nbsp;
per second. But, by 1983, this would be increased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:48.300
all the way to 50 meters per second. This&nbsp;
actually meant that producing fiber optic cables&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:53.880
was cheaper than producing traditional copper&nbsp;
cables. But, despite these revolutionary findings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:59.580
the broadband industry would head in a completely&nbsp;
different direction: Dial-up internet. If you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:04.500
not familiar with dial up, it consists of calling&nbsp;
a specified phone number and getting internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:12.420
connection through your phone cable. This resulted&nbsp;
in extremely slow speeds of 56 kbps or lower. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:17.640
this was actually for the best. When you’re&nbsp;
introducing a new technology, you want to reduce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:22.920
barriers to adoption by as much as possible. So,&nbsp;
the fact that the internet could work through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:29.040
phone lines that already existed around the world&nbsp;
was a massive win. But, over time, this quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:35.040
turned into the internet's biggest inhibitor&nbsp;
as well. You see, ISPs figured, why create new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:41.040
infrastructure if we don’t have to. And this&nbsp;
led to the broadband industry becoming fixated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:48.060
on two offerings: DSL and Cable. DSL was offered&nbsp;
by telephone providers and aimed to leverage phone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:53.700
lines as much as possible. Meanwhile, cable&nbsp;
internet was offered by cable providers and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:59.340
aimed to leverage cable lines as much as possible.&nbsp;
There was also a third medium of internet which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:05.100
sought to leverage satellite infrastructure, but&nbsp;
this never really took off resulting in a duopoly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:11.220
between DSL and cable providers. Sometimes, DSL&nbsp;
providers made some sort of advancement and edged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:17.160
out cable providers. Other times, cable providers&nbsp;
made some sort of advancement and edged out DSL&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:23.940
providers. But, neither was radically better than&nbsp;
the other. Also, these so called advancements were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:33.300
by no means breakthroughs. They usually consisted&nbsp;
of improvements from 1 Mbps to 2 MPBS or 2 Mbps to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:41.520
4 Mbps. While these were admittedly 2X increases,&nbsp;
they were nothing in comparison to the 100, 500,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:47.460
1000x increases that were possible through fiber.&nbsp;
There was a sort of unspoken agreement between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:52.380
the DSL providers and the cable providers that&nbsp;
they would both stick to their lanes and not do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:58.740
anything to ambitious. And given that no startup&nbsp;
could challenge their dominance, these ISPs pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:05.160
much set the rules. But, no one really cared&nbsp;
either. Virtually no one needed speeds of 300,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:12.060
200, or even 100 Mbps in the 1990s and 2000s.&nbsp;
I mean, all people were doing with the internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:18.720
was forums, emails, and light social media.&nbsp;
But, all of this would change after 2010.

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:23.280
THE NEED FOR SPEED:&nbsp;

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:28.320
While ISPs had stuck to linear improvements,&nbsp;
companies based on the internet were growing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:34.920
exponentially and so was their need for speed.&nbsp;
Likely the two best examples of this are streaming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:41.280
and gaming. 4K TVs have become dirt cheap and&nbsp;
basically every streaming platforms offers 4K&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:47.520
options whether it be YouTube or Netflix. But,&nbsp;
the biggest bottleneck to making 4K streaming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:55.920
a reality was broadband internet. To stream&nbsp;
480p, you only need 1.1 Mbps, but to stream 4K,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.920 --> 00:07:03.540
you need 20 Mbps. And that’s if you’re the only&nbsp;
person using the internet in your household. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:10.080
you live with your family or roommates, you need&nbsp;
speeds of 50 or 100 Mbps for everyone to be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:15.840
to use the internet comfortably. The same thing&nbsp;
applies to gaming as well given that the industry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:23.040
was moving away from physical copies. To download&nbsp;
these new 100 or 150 GB games, you needed much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:31.560
faster speeds than 4 Mbps. At that rate, it would&nbsp;
literally take days to download these games. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:36.720
atleast that’s a one time problem. To actually&nbsp;
enjoy many of these online games, you need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:41.580
reliable ping which simply wasn’t feasible&nbsp;
through the traditional offerings. Clearly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:48.934
it was time for disruption, but ISPs weren’t&nbsp;
gonna disrupt themselves. So, enter Google.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.934 --> 00:07:53.340
Google’s incentives for radically improving&nbsp;
internet speeds were obvious. I mean, they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.340 --> 00:07:58.620
the largest internet company in the world. If&nbsp;
anyone has stakes in the internet becoming better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.620 --> 00:08:05.640
it’s them. And it’s not just for mindlessly&nbsp;
watching YouTube videos in 4K. That right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:10.620
there was probably the least of their concerns.&nbsp;
Their real concern was productive applications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:15.780
like video calls through Google Hangouts, virtual&nbsp;
classrooms through Chrome OS and Google Classroom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:22.440
and most importantly the cloud through Google&nbsp;
Cloud. If they wanted any of these applications to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:29.640
take off, they needed ISPs to ditch DSL and cable&nbsp;
and switch to fiber or something even better. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:35.100
as a result, they would step up to the plate&nbsp;
with Google Fiber. And they went straight for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:40.680
the big guns. They didn’t even bother aiming for&nbsp;
numbers with units of Mb. They went straight for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:46.260
1 Gbps internet. Keep in mind, this was during&nbsp;
a time when most people were struggling to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.260 --> 00:08:55.620
speeds of 1 Mbps. So, speeds of 1 Gbps were very&nbsp;
much unfathomable, atleast for me. For Google,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.620 --> 00:09:01.800
these speeds were nothing given that it took a lot&nbsp;
more horsepower to run their massive data centers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:09.360
networks, and servers. For perspective, Google&nbsp;
needed 1 Pbps or 1 million Gbps to run their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:17.640
Jupiter network way back in 2015. Who&nbsp;
knows how much they need today. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:23.160
theoretically speaking, offering fiber shouldn’t&nbsp;
have been that difficult in terms of technology.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:27.180
Google just had to make a fraction of&nbsp;
what they were capable of available to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:33.720
the end consumers. But, as we so often find,&nbsp;
theory doesn’t always translate to reality.

00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:35.364
SANDBAGGING FIBER:
Google didn’t approach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.364 --> 00:09:38.880
fiber naively though. They very much knew just&nbsp;
how difficult it would be to challenge ISPs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:45.840
That didn’t matter though because they had no&nbsp;
intention of actually overtaking said ISPs. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:51.180
wait a minute, if Google was gonna get into the&nbsp;
broadband game, they might as well try to grow a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:58.440
new sector and displace the legacy players right?&nbsp;
Well, the answer is actually absolutely not from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:05.760
a financial perspective. Here’s the thing, it cost&nbsp;
AT&amp;T $140 billion to accomplish a semi nationwide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:11.700
fiber rollout. And that’s despite their decades of&nbsp;
experience, hundreds of billions of dollars worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.700 --> 00:10:18.480
of existing infrastructure, and telecommunications&nbsp;
being their primary focus and business. If Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:24.600
were to try to achieve this from scratch, it&nbsp;
would cost them $200 or $300 billion especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:30.360
given Google’s top of the market salaries. But,&nbsp;
even if we assumed that they were somehow able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:37.020
get it done for a $100 billion, it still makes&nbsp;
no sense. You see, Google’s entire market cap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.020 --> 00:10:44.160
back in 2010 was only $300 to $400 billion. This&nbsp;
means that if they actually took fiber seriously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:49.620
they would essentially be betting a third of&nbsp;
their entire net worth on this market. And much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.620 --> 00:10:55.620
of this would probably have be paid for using&nbsp;
debt. That itself is a pretty bad situation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.620 --> 00:11:02.380
but it gets exponentially worse when you consider&nbsp;
opportunity cost. Take YouTube for example. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.380 --> 00:11:07.620
is no official marketcap for YouTube, but I&nbsp;
think we can all agree that YouTube is worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.620 --> 00:11:15.300
atleast a $100 billion. But, do you know what&nbsp;
Google paid for YouTube? A mere $1.65 billion. An&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:21.240
even better example is Android. Again, I think we&nbsp;
can all agree that Android is worth atleast a $100&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.240 --> 00:11:27.960
billion. But, do you know what Google paid&nbsp;
for Andoird? A disgusting $50 million. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.960 --> 00:11:35.040
with a $100 billion, Google can either bet on&nbsp;
60 potential YouTubes, 2000 potential Androids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:42.060
or they can try to challenge AT&amp;T and takeover&nbsp;
the broadband market. Even if Google Fiber grew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.060 --> 00:11:49.620
to become the entire size of AT&amp;T, that’s only&nbsp;
$135 billion. In other words, Google can either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.620 --> 00:11:57.900
bet on creating 60 YouTubes, 2000 Androids,&nbsp;
or 1 AT&amp;T. For obvious reasons, even a fool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.900 --> 00:12:04.320
wouldn’t choose the AT&amp;T option. And that’s why&nbsp;
Google created Fiber knowing that it would likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:10.800
never blow up. But, that didn’t matter. Google&nbsp;
Fiber didn’t need to blow up to be successful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:16.800
It just needed to threaten the monopolies&nbsp;
of ISPs, and that’s exactly what it did.

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:24.540
THE STATE OF THE INTERNET:
The ISPs fired back so hard that they’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.540 --> 00:12:32.880
actually started to out offer Google Fiber. Google&nbsp;
only offers up to 2 Gbps while AT&amp;T offers up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:40.200
to 5 Gbps, granted it is quite expensive. The&nbsp;
same thing can be said about Verizon, Xfinity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.200 --> 00:12:46.620
Spectrum, Century Link, Optimum, and basically&nbsp;
any other ISP you can think of. We should also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:52.800
mention that previously unthinkable breatkhroughs&nbsp;
have come about regarding cable internet. Somehow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.800 --> 00:13:00.600
the same cable providers are now able to&nbsp;
provide speeds of up to 500 Mbps. In the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:06.360
Google Fiber was never meant to become a household&nbsp;
name when it comes to broadband internet. Even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.360 --> 00:13:11.580
if Google was capable of accomplishing something&nbsp;
like this, it simply wouldn’t be worth the cost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.580 --> 00:13:16.740
effort, and time required to actually&nbsp;
make that vision a reality. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.740 --> 00:13:23.940
what Google could accomplish with relatively low&nbsp;
effort was reignite the stagnant ISP industry. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.940 --> 00:13:28.800
looking at the results, it appears that Google&nbsp;
Fiber was a bigger success than their wildest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.800 --> 00:13:34.620
dreams. Did you realize what game Google was&nbsp;
playing with fiber? Comment that down below. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.620 --> 00:13:39.660
drop a like if you’re glad that ISPs are&nbsp;
finally doing something. And of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

